ga25(){return"Okey 101 Rules"},
ga21(){return"Tiles"},
ga24(){return"The game uses 106 tiles: numbers 1 to 13 in four colours (red, yellow, black, blue), two copies each (104 tiles), plus 2 false jokers."},
ga20(){return"Deal"},
ga23(){return"Tiles are shuffled, one indicator is revealed, then the deal happens. Each player gets 21 tiles, the starting player 22. Exactly 20 tiles remain in the draw pile. The indicator is never drawable. Turn order is counter-clockwise: the player before you sits on your left, the next player on your right."},
ga1X(){return"Okey and false joker"},
ga1R(){return"If the indicator is (colour C, number N), the okey is N+1 of the same colour. If the indicator is 13, the okey is 1 of the same colour. This wrap only derives the okey; runs never wrap from 13 to 1. The okey is wild and counts as the face value of the tile it replaces. The 2 false jokers act as the indicator tile itself and are not wild."},
ga1W(){return"Sets and runs"},
ga1Q(){return"Run: at least 3 consecutive tiles of one colour. 12-13-1 and 13-1-2 are invalid. A run laid on the table may hold at most 5 tiles; a table run may be extended later. Set: 3 or 4 tiles of the same number, all different colours. A meld may use at most 1 joker and may never be made of jokers only."},
ga1Y(){return"Opening"},
ga1S(){return"To open you must lay at least 101 points in a single turn. Face values are summed; 11-11-11 is 33 points. A joker counts as the tile it replaces. Before opening you may not add to anyone's melds."},
ga1Z(){return"Pairs opening"},
ga1T(){return"A mutually exclusive alternative to normal opening. A pair is two tiles of the same colour and number. You open by laying at least 5 pairs at once. A player on the pairs path may not mix in runs or sets and may not add to others' melds. You win with 11 pairs (22 tiles); the 22nd tile completes the 11th pair and nothing is discarded. The okey may complete a pair, and two false jokers pair with each other."},
ga22(){return"Turn structure"},
ga26(){return"1) Draw exactly one tile, from the face-down pile or the top of your left neighbour's discard pile. 2) Optionally open, or add to melds if you already opened. 3) Discard exactly one tile to your own pile and end the turn with 21 tiles. A tile taken from a discard pile may not be discarded back on the same turn. The okey may be discarded, and your right-hand neighbour may take it."},
ga1V(){return"Finish types"},
ga1P(){return"Normal: opened earlier, then discards the last tile. Head: opens and finishes in a single turn from a full 22-tile hand, never having opened before - the 101 threshold still applies. Pairs: finishing via the 11-pairs path. With okey: the final discarded tile is the okey. Okey head: a head finish whose final discard is the okey. Pairs with okey: the okey completes the 11th pair."},
ga2_(){return"Scoring"},
ga1U(){return"Lowest cumulative score wins; points are penalties and the hand winner goes negative. Tiles left on the rack are written down at face value. An okey left in hand counts 25, a false joker counts the indicator's number. A player who never opened takes a flat penalty from the table. If the deck runs out with nobody finishing there is no winner, no flat penalty is applied, and everyone simply writes their deadwood."},
